markdown <<MARKDOWN
# TAG Protocol for Meaningful Feedback

When teams are ready for feedback (typically around Day 8 / Milestone 4), use the TAG protocol to guide structured, meaningful peer feedback.


TAG helps students move beyond surface-level comments and engage in feedback that improves both the product and their thinking.

---

## How TAG Works

<div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; max-width: 900px; margin: auto;">

  <!-- TELL -->
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
    <img src="https://images.code.org/b7b707ce75276f775ec583954ec65bf4-image-1776956980531.png" alt="icon for stength with fist" style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; height: auto; border-radius: 10px;">
    <div style="flex: 1; background: #F5F7F8; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; font-size: 14pt;">
      <p style="font-size: 18pt;"><b>1. Tell: Identify Strengths</b></p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">
        Students highlight something that is working well in the app.
        <br><br>
        This reinforces strong ideas and helps teams recognize what to keep.
      </p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;"><i><b>Goal:</b> Build confidence and clarity around what is already effective.</i></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ASK -->
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
    <img src="https://images.code.org/6b33a4aff94907563a6ed75218fae485-image-1776957078956.png" alt="Icon with quesiton marks" style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; height: auto; border-radius: 10px;">
    <div style="flex: 1; background: #F5F7F8; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; font-size: 14pt;">
      <p style="font-size: 18pt;"><b>2. Ask: Clarify Understanding</b></p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">
        Students ask questions about how the app works or why decisions were made.
        <br><br>
        This helps uncover confusion or gaps in communication.
      </p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;"><i><b>Goal:</b> Surface misunderstandings and prompt deeper thinking.</i></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- GIVE -->
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
    <img src="https://images.code.org/948548ff076e40fbdaec850ae53eb3e9-image-1776957143962.png" alt="lightbulb with suggestions icon" style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; height: auto; border-radius: 10px;">
    <div style="flex: 1; background: #F5F7F8; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; font-size: 14pt;">
      <p style="font-size: 18pt;"><b>3. Give: Suggest Improvements</b></p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">
        Students offer a concrete suggestion to improve the app.
        <br><br>
        Suggestions should be specific and actionable.
      </p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;"><i><b>Goal:</b> Move the project forward with clear next steps.</i></p>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>

---

## When to Use TAG

Students do <b>not</b> need a finished app to get feedback.

<div style="background-color: #F5F7F8; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%;">
  <p style="font-size: 14pt;">
    As soon as a team has <b>one working interaction</b>, they are ready for feedback.
    <br><br>
    Early feedback leads to better decisions and prevents wasted work later.
  </p>
</div>

---

## Keep TAG Flexible

TAG does not need to be a rigid, whole-class activity.

- Teams can give feedback to a neighboring group  
- You can use TAG during teacher spot-checks  
- Students can adapt TAG when reviewing AI-generated feedback  

The goal is consistent, meaningful feedback, not a specific format.

---

## Feedback is a Decision, Not a To-Do List

<div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%;">
  <p style="font-size: 14pt;">
    Strong teams treat feedback as <b>information for decision-making</b>, not a checklist.
  </p>
</div>

Coach students to:

- Act on feedback that improves their goals  
- Defer feedback that isn’t immediately useful  
- Reject feedback that doesn’t align with their design  

When reviewing work, look for evidence that teams explain <b>why</b> they accepted or rejected feedback in their Project Guide.

---

<div style="text-align: center; width: 80%; margin: 0 auto;">
  <p style="
    font-size: 18px; 
    background-color: #F5F7F8; 
    padding: 10px; 
    border-left: 5px solid #048899; 
    border-right: 5px solid #048899;
    display: inline-block;
  ">
    <i>TAG turns feedback into a structured conversation that helps teams improve their work and make better decisions.</i>
  </p>
</div>

